WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.280
Dota 2 is a game of complex choices,

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.100
thanks to the countless interactions between hero abilities and items.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.620
Now, just one of those abilities can be the difference between winning and losing a team fight.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.460
And when it comes to the big leagues, even the pros mess up sometimes.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:29.040
So with The International coming soon, we thought this would be the perfect time to bring you the top 10 misplays in Dota 2.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:36.480
We thought we'd kick off our list with a player that is known for his unfortunate mishaps Eternal Envy.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:45.520
Squaring off against PD during a qualifier for the Boston Major 2016, Envy decided that he’d try to solo Roshan.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:54.140
(Casting)

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.040
And it went exactly as you thought.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:02.680
Envy was furiously trying to strike down Rosh before the enemy team could rotate and kill him.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.780
But with only a sliver of health left on the Rosh,

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:11.760
the last swipe of its claw killed Envy, and the enemy team even got a chance to kill him.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:31.420
(Casting)

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.380
Talk about a rough day at the office.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:40.460
Sometimes in esports, a play is so bad that it immortalizes the mitsake for years to come,

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.720
which is why one of Dota’s most famous examples of this comes in at number nine on our list.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.380
During a match between CLG and  M5 during The International 2’s Group Stage,

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:55.400
M5’s PGG tried to land a blackhole to swing a fight in his team’s favour

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.060
and, well … he quite literally missed everyone.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:22.660
(Casting)

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:28.380
From that moment on, whiffed blackholes would be forever known as a “PGG blackhole.”

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:33.660
At the core of most catastrophic misplays, is simply a lack of communication.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.260
And nothing illustrates that more than our number eight misplay

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:45.880
OG were ganking LFY’s Batrider as Ana popped his Omnislash on Inflame to secure what should have been an easy kill,

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:50.720
knowing his teammates were nearby. Unfortunately, JerAx was on another page,

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:57.800
and popped his Eul’s to hold the Batrider in place which cancelled out the damage Ana was hoping to deal,

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:17.380
(Casting)

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.740
In the end, Inflame was able to pop his BKB and get away safely.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:31.640
During a TI5 match between Virtus.pro and Complexity, Swindlezz taught us an all important lesson about dropping your items, just don’t do it.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:36.760
Our number seven misplay is that fateful moment when Swindlezz dropped his Eul’s Scepter

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.780
in an attempt to eke out a little bit of extra mana to fight with.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:52.580
(Casting)

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:56.920
That unfortunate play marked the end for Complexity’s run at TI 5.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.740
During the finals of ESL One: Hamburg 2017,

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:07.520
Secret had their backs to the wall against Virtus.pro and needed to pull off a big play to get back in the game.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.860
So Fata hit a 2-man reverse polarity on both of VP’s carries

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:17.820
and midone sent 3 remnants to the enemy’s location in the hopes of dishing out huge amounts of damage.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.340
At least that was the plan.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:33.540
(Casting)

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:38.520
What actually happened was a bout of miscommunication that saw Fata skewer the enemy stack,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:45.140
midone miss his ult, and their chance at pulling off a comeback come to a less than dramatic end.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.800
Everybody knows just how good Universe and Evil Geniuses were at their peak.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:56.160
But as our number five misplay proves, even the mighty are sometimes humbled by some sloppy play.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:04.360
And that’s exactly what happened at 2017’s Kiev Major, when Zai made this catastrophic blunder on IO

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:14.180
Zai used his ultimate to teleport Universe’s Legion Commander in for a gank-attempt which by all accounts is expert usage of the two heroes’ ultimates

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:23.000
that is until Zai forgot to untether from Universe transporting him back to his original location and his sudden death.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:43.940
(Casting)

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.040
OG would go on to beat EG 2-0 and eliminate them from the major.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:54.920
Simply put, the ESL One: New York qualifiers in 2015 were not kind to funn1k.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:57.140
He whiffed on not one,

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:01.220
(Casting)

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:02.240
not two,

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:10.140
(Casting)

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.080
but three Enigma ulti's

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:21.560
(Casting)

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:25.680
to cost his team a chance to play on the big stage in the big apple.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:32.380
Team Liquid’s run at TI 7 was a historic moment for both the organization and for Kuroky.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:40.740
But our number three misplay should remind you that things could have gone differently if it wasn’t for a major blunder from Newbee in game number 2.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:47.320
Taking on TL in the grand final, Newbee were ahead and making a high ground push looking to get on the scoreboard

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:53.220
but after a blown Kpii ultimate, things started to go downhill for the Chinese squad.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:07:08.040
(Casting)

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.960
Linken’s Sphere gobbled up the ulti from Kpii and Newbee would immediately lose rax

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.280
because of this mishap which would eventually lead to their loss of the second game.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:24.720
At this point Newbee simply could not stop Liquid’s momentum, losing their chance at the Aegis of Champions.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:31.240
During the 2015 Asia Championships Team Secret and Cloud9 were locked in a heated playoff affair.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.200
But, as Cloud9 looked to be taking control — things got a bit awkward to say the least.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:42.640
Essentially C9 collectively forgot that they didn’t have any teleports available

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:49.280
and Secret were happy to sneak up behind them and decimate their barracks before the boys in blue could react.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:53.320
There’s, just nothing more to say about this one.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:08:41.760
(Casting)

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:49.120
Here it is, the bummer of the ball, king stinker, the worst Dota 2 misplay of all-time.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:58.180
And well, I bet you probably saw this one coming, it's Kyxy’s Aegis Deny at The International 3 in 2013.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:03.000
In Game 3 of the Lower Bracket final Na’Vi was facing off against Orange

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:10.240
and, well Orange was rolling over them. They were able to snowball some early team fights and set up to take Roshan.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.420
Then it happened.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:18.980
Kyxy denied the Aegis of the Immortal from Mushi and Na’Vi saw an opportunity to pounce.

00:09:18.980 --> 00:10:11.260
(Casting)

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:18.640
In what was the biggest mistake in TI history, the Europeans managed a crazy come from behind victory.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:23.300
Guys we produce great content just like this each and every week,

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:27.800
so if you’re not subscribed to the channel already, well, that’s the real misplay.

